---
layout: base.njk
title: Help
description: How to use Prospektor — guides, answers and a search box, served straight from the running product so they always describe the version that is live.
# One hub per EDITION of the corpus (#535): the English one at /help/, and
# /<code>/help/ for every language the studio holds at least one guide in —
# src/_data/help.js decides the list from the studio's own answer, never from
# a list kept here. German and Dutch get no hub until the studio ships a guide
# in them; the nav's Help item then falls back to /help/ by the localize rule.
pagination:
  data: help.editions
  size: 1
  alias: edition
  addAllPagesToCollections: true
permalink: "{{ edition.prefix }}/help/"
faqs:
  - q: What does Prospektor cost?
    a: $999 a month per workspace, taken by Stripe. The scan on the home page is free and needs no sign-up — point it at a site and it reads that company before it says a word about them.
  - q: Do I have to install anything?
    a: No. Prospektor runs in the browser at studio.prospektor.ai. There is nothing to download and no extension to add.
  - q: Can I run Prospektor for my clients?
    a: Yes. An agency-flagged workspace gets New client workspace in the workspace switcher, for the agency's owner or admins — name the client and a researched workspace exists before the next screen. A portfolio upload creates a whole roster at once. Each client workspace is billed as its own workspace.
    link: /help/workspace/
    linkLabel: Read the workspace guide
  - q: Who at my company can sign in?
    a: If you bought with a company address, the access list carries your whole domain as well as your own address, so everyone at that company can sign in — including addresses that do not exist yet. An owner can keep that or narrow it to named addresses.
    link: /help/workspace/
    linkLabel: Members and access
  - q: What happens if I stop paying?
    a: The workspace pauses rather than disappears. The lock screen's resubscribe button reopens the same studio with everything kept. Deleting a workspace is a separate, deliberate act in the danger zone, only an owner may do it, and it cannot be undone.
  - q: What do you do with my data?
    a: The privacy page is the full answer, and it is written to be read rather than to be survived. In short - the studio holds no card details, sets only the cookies that sign you in, and makes no third-party requests.
    link: /privacy/
    linkLabel: Read the privacy page
---

<header class="help-hero">
  <div class="help-hero-inner">
    <span class="tag">{% t %}help{% endt %}</span>
    <h1 class="help-h1">{% t %}How can we help?{% endt %}</h1>
    <p class="help-lede">{% t %}Guides and step-by-step answers for Prospektor — each on its own page, all searchable from here. They read the same documentation the product itself runs on, so they always describe the version that is live.{% endt %}</p>
    <div class="help-search">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="7" cy="7" r="5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M11 11l3.2 3.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
      <input id="helpSearch" type="search" placeholder="{% t %}Search the guides — try &ldquo;how do I create a new workspace&rdquo;{% endt %}" autocomplete="off" aria-label="{% t %}Search the help guides{% endt %}">
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

{#- The search results take the page over while a query is live: the cards and
    the guides below are both hidden, and this is the only panel on screen. -#}
<section id="helpResults" class="help-results" aria-live="polite" hidden></section>

{#- ── the hub: a card per guide, each linking to its own page ──
    Cards are built at build time from the corpus (#136) and rebuilt by help.js
    only if the live corpus has actually moved since — see the hash on
    #helpGuides. The grid is the one #144 shipped for /resources/; the CSS is
    shared rather than copied.

    Since #166 a card is a link to /help/<slug>/ rather than an anchor into a
    stack of eleven guides on this page. Since #535 the link is the guide's
    page in THIS edition's language, and a guide the studio has not translated
    yet says so on its card rather than surprising the reader on the page. -#}
<section id="helpHub" class="help-hub">
  {%- if edition.guides.length %}
  <ul class="card-grid">
    {%- for g in edition.guides %}
    <li class="card">
      <a href="{{ edition.prefix }}/help/{{ g.slug }}/">
        <span class="card-emoji" aria-hidden="true">{{ g.emoji }}</span>
        <span class="card-topic">{{ g.topic | t }}{% if g.language != edition.code %} · <span lang="{{ g.language }}">{{ 'not yet in {language}' | t({ language: edition.own }) }}</span>{% endif %}</span>
        <h2 class="card-title"{% if g.language != edition.code %} lang="{{ g.language }}"{% endif %}>{{ g.title }}</h2>
        <p class="card-dek"{% if g.language != edition.code %} lang="{{ g.language }}"{% endif %}>{{ g.dek }}</p>
        <span class="card-cta">{% t %}Read the guide{% endt %} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
      </a>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
  {%- else %}
  {#- The corpus could not be reached at build time. The page still ships and
      help.js fills this in from the live studio — see src/_data/help.js. -#}
  <p class="help-dim" id="helpHubEmpty">{% t %}Loading the guides…{% endt %}</p>
  {%- endif %}
</section>

{#- ── guides with no page of their own ──
    Normally empty, and that is the point.

    #136 put every guide's text in this element, because a crawler was handed
    the word "Loading…" and ten guides of long-tail content were invisible.
    #166 moved that text to /help/<slug>/, where a guide can rank for the
    question it answers instead of eleven guides competing as one URL. So the
    crawlable copy did not go away — it moved, and the sitemap follows it.

    What is left here is the case #166 had to answer rather than accept: the
    studio can add a guide at any moment, and #76's property is that it shows
    on this site the moment the studio deploys, with no website build in
    between. A guide the last build never saw has no page, so help.js renders
    it INLINE here and points its card at the anchor. It reads immediately; it
    gets its own URL at the next build.

    `data-pages` is how the browser tells the two apart: the slugs that got a
    page in this build. `data-corpus-hash` is #136's no-double-render stamp,
    unchanged. `data-lang` and `data-prefix` are this edition's (#535): help.js
    asks the studio for the corpus in that language and writes every link it
    hands out under that prefix. -#}
<section id="helpGuides" class="help-guides"
         data-corpus-hash="{{ edition.hash }}"
         data-corpus-source="{{ edition.source }}"
         data-pages="{{ edition.slugs | join(' ') }}"
         data-lang="{{ edition.code }}"
         data-lang-name="{{ edition.own }}"
         data-prefix="{{ edition.prefix }}"></section>

<section class="help-faq">
  <div class="help-faq-inner">
    <h2 class="help-faq-h2">{% t %}Frequently asked questions{% endt %}</h2>
    <dl class="help-faq-list">
      {%- for item in faqs %}
      <div class="help-faq-item">
        <dt>{{ item.q | t }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ item.a | t }}{% if item.link %} <a href="{{ item.link | localize }}">{{ item.linkLabel | t }} →</a>{% endif %}</dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="help-support">
  <div class="help-support-inner">
    <span class="tag">{% t %}still stuck{% endt %}</span>
    <h2>{% t %}Ask a person.{% endt %}</h2>
    <p>{% t %}The <strong>Support</strong> pill inside the studio answers in seconds and already knows which workspace you are in. If you would rather write, we read every mail.{% endt %}</p>
    <a class="btn-cta" href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>
    <p class="help-support-alt"><a href="https://studio.prospektor.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{% t %}Open the studio ↗{% endt %}</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

{#- The visible FAQ above and this are rendered from the same frontmatter list,
    so the structured data cannot drift from what the page says. -#}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {%- for item in faqs %}
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": {{ (item.q | t) | dump | safe }},
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": {{ (item.a | t) | dump | safe }} }
    }{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
    {%- endfor %}
  ]
}
</script>

{#- The corpus the build rendered from, verbatim, for help.js: it is what the
    search index is built from before any network call, and what the live
    corpus is compared against so the page is not re-rendered for nothing. -#}
<script type="application/json" id="helpCorpus">{{ edition.corpusJson | safe }}</script>

{#- defer (#170, the remainder of #137's pass). These two are what that
    pass deliberately left out, and here is why: #136 stamps a corpus hash on
    #helpGuides specifically to stop the browser rendering a second time over
    what the build already rendered, and that guarantee had not been
    re-verified with `defer` on. It has been now, and it holds — because the
    stamp is a comparison of two hashes, not a race. help.js reads
    data-corpus-hash off an element the parser is guaranteed to have finished
    with, and does nothing at all when the live corpus agrees with it.
    `defer` makes that guarantee stronger rather than weaker: the element the
    script looks up is certain to exist, where before it was merely likely to.

    Order still holds, which is the other thing that had to be true: deferred
    scripts run in document order, so help-render.js still defines
    window.HelpRender before help.js reads it. help.js already returns if it
    does not.

    This is the heaviest page on the site — 85 KB of HTML, most of it the
    embedded corpus that makes search answer with the studio down — so it is
    where a parser stop cost most. Driven in test/drive.js §7 and §7c(b);
    pinned for every page by test/pages.test.js. -#}
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/help-render.js' | asset }}" defer></script>
<script src="{{ '/assets/js/help.js' | asset }}" defer></script>
